{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_active.go | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/start_lock_linux.go | sed -n '1,105p'; rg -n \\\"review_process|reap|POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT|process-group termination|Linux|Windows|descendant|run shim|let every command|finish before ending|command.*finish\\\" POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_active.go | sed -n '1,340p'; nl -ba pose-mcp/internal/pose/start_lock_linux.go | sed -n '1,105p'; rg -n \\\"review_process|reap|POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT|process-group termination|Linux|Windows|descendant|run shim|let every command|finish before ending|command.*finish\\\" POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tpackage pose\n     2\t\n     3\t// Active delegated runs and cancellation (spec pose-delegation-surface R13).\n     4\t//\n     5\t// A run's record is written when it ends, so a running run is known only by a\n     6\t// private marker its process writes when the attempt starts and removes when\n     7\t// the record is published. The marker names the process the way a start lock\n     8\t// does, so a reader can tell a running owner from a dead one. Cancelling\n     9\t// writes a request beside the marker; the running process watches for it,\n    10\t// stops the agent and records the run failed with the cause cancelled. The\n    11\t// attempt stays consumed: cancelling never returns budget.\n    12\t\n    13\timport (\n    14\t\t\"context\"\n    15\t\t\"crypto/rand\"\n    16\t\t\"encoding/hex\"\n    17\t\t\"encoding/json\"\n    18\t\t\"errors\"\n    19\t\t\"fmt\"\n    20\t\t\"os\"\n    21\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n    22\t\t\"sort\"\n    23\t\t\"strings\"\n    24\t\t\"sync\"\n    25\t\t\"sync/atomic\"\n    26\t\t\"time\"\n    27\t)\n    28\t\n    29\tconst delegationActiveDir = reviewPrivateDir + \"/active\"\n    30\t\n    31\t// DelegationActiveRun is the marker of a run in progress.\n    32\ttype DelegationActiveRun struct {\n    33\t\tSchemaVersion int    `json:\"schema_version\"`\n    34\t\tAttemptID     string `json:\"attempt_id\"`\n    35\t\t// RunToken names this run of the attempt: a cancellation request carries\n    36\t\t// it, so a request meant for one run never stops another.\n    37\t\tRunToken  string         `json:\"run_token\"`\n    38\t\tResumes   string         `json:\"resumes,omitempty\"`\n    39\t\tLabel     string         `json:\"label,omitempty\"`\n    40\t\tPurpose   string         `json:\"purpose\"`\n    41\t\tAgent     string         `json:\"agent\"`\n    42\t\tScope     string         `json:\"scope\"`\n    43\t\tBundleID  string         `json:\"bundle_id\"`\n    44\t\tStartedAt string         `json:\"started_at\"`\n    45\t\tOwner     StartLockOwner `json:\"owner\"`\n    46\t\t// HeartbeatAt is rewritten every HeartbeatEvery seconds by the process\n    47\t\t// that runs the attempt (spec pose-delegated-review-completion-signal R4,\n    48\t\t// D2).\n    49\t\tHeartbeatAt    string `json:\"heartbeat_at,omitempty\"`\n    50\t\tHeartbeatEvery int    `json:\"heartbeat_every,omitempty\"`\n    51\t\t// StalledAt is computed by the reader from the stall claim the first\n    52\t\t// reader that found the run stalled published.\n    53\t\tStalledAt string `json:\"stalled_at,omitempty\"`\n    54\t\t// Liveness is computed by the reader: owner-active, owner-dead or owner-unknown.\n    55\t\tLiveness string `json:\"liveness,omitempty\"`\n    56\t\tDetail   string `json:\"detail,omitempty\"`\n    57\t\t// CancelRequested is computed by the reader.\n    58\t\tCancelRequested bool `json:\"cancel_requested,omitempty\"`\n    59\t\t// Round is the review round the run reviews (spec\n    60\t\t// pose-delegated-review-round-protocol R1).\n    61\t\tRound int `json:\"round,omitempty\"`\n    62\t}\n    63\t\n    64\tfunc (s Store) delegationActiveDir(create bool) (string, error) {\n    65\t\tif create {\n    66\t\t\tif _, err := ensureReviewArtifactDir(s.Root, reviewPrivateDir, true); err != nil {\n    67\t\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n    68\t\t\t}\n    69\t\t\tdir := filepath.Join(s.Root, filepath.FromSlash(reviewPrivateDir))\n    70\t\t\tignore := filepath.Join(dir, \".gitignore\")\n    71\t\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(ignore); errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n    72\t\t\t\tif err := PublishFile(ignore, []byte(\"*\\n\"), 0600, PublishAuthoritative, PublishExpectation{Mode: ExpectAbsent}); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, os.ErrExist) {\n    73\t\t\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n    74\t\t\t\t}\n    75\t\t\t}\n    76\t\t}\n    77\t\treturn ensureReviewArtifactDir(s.Root, delegationActiveDir, create)\n    78\t}\n    79\t\n    80\tfunc validAttemptFileName(attempt string) bool {\n    81\t\treturn attempt != \"\" && len(attempt) <= 128 && !strings.ContainsAny(attempt, `/\\.`+\"\\x00\")\n    82\t}\n    83\t\n    84\t// markDelegationActive writes the marker and starts the cancellation watcher.\n    85\t// The returned stop removes both; cancelled reports whether a cancellation\n    86\t// request stopped the run.\n    87\t//\n    88\t// stop(true) stops the watcher and the heartbeat but keeps the marker: a run\n    89\t// whose record was not published leaves it, and it turns stalled once its\n    90\t// process is gone (spec pose-delegated-review-completion-signal R4, R5).\n    91\tfunc (s Store) markDelegationActive(run ReviewRun, cancel context.CancelFunc) (stop func(keepMarker ...bool), cancelled *atomic.Bool, err error) {\n    92\t\tcancelled = &atomic.Bool{}\n    93\t\tif !validAttemptFileName(run.AttemptID) {\n    94\t\t\treturn func(...bool) {}, cancelled, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: attempt %q cannot name an active marker\", run.AttemptID)\n    95\t\t}\n    96\t\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(true)\n    97\t\tif err != nil {\n    98\t\t\treturn func(...bool) {}, cancelled, err\n    99\t\t}\n   100\t\towner := StartLockOwner{SchemaVersion: StartLockOwnerSchemaVersion, Resource: \"delegate:\" + run.AttemptID, Worktree: s.Root, Operation: \"pose delegate \" + run.Purpose, PID: os.Getpid(), AcquiredAt: run.StartedAt}\n   101\t\towner.Host, _ = os.Hostname()\n   102\t\tidentifyStartLockProcess(&owner)\n   103\t\ttoken := make([]byte, 16)\n   104\t\tif _, err := rand.Read(token); err != nil {\n   105\t\t\treturn func(...bool) {}, cancelled, err\n   106\t\t}\n   107\t\tevery := delegationHeartbeatEvery\n   108\t\tmarker := DelegationActiveRun{SchemaVersion: 1, AttemptID: run.AttemptID, RunToken: hex.EncodeToString(token), Resumes: run.Resumes, Label: run.Label, Purpose: run.Purpose, Agent: run.Adapter, Scope: run.Scope, BundleID: run.BundleID, StartedAt: run.StartedAt, Owner: owner, Round: run.Round,\n   109\t\t\tHeartbeatAt: time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano), HeartbeatEvery: int(every / time.Second)}\n   110\t\traw, _ := json.MarshalIndent(marker, \"\", \"  \")\n   111\t\tpath := filepath.Join(dir, run.AttemptID+\".json\")\n   112\t\trequest := cancelRequestPath(dir, marker)\n   113\t\tif err := PublishFile(path, append(raw, '\\n'), 0600, PublishAuthoritative, PublishExpectation{Mode: ExpectAbsent}); err != nil {\n   114\t\t\treturn func(...bool) {}, cancelled, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: another run of attempt %s is active: %w\", run.AttemptID, err)\n   115\t\t}\n   116\t\tdone, exited := make(chan struct{}), make(chan struct{})\n   117\t\tgo func() {\n   118\t\t\tdefer close(exited)\n   119\t\t\tticker := time.NewTicker(250 * time.Millisecond)\n   120\t\t\tdefer ticker.Stop()\n   121\t\t\theartbeat := time.NewTicker(every)\n   122\t\t\tdefer heartbeat.Stop()\n   123\t\t\tfor {\n   124\t\t\t\tselect {\n   125\t\t\t\tcase <-done:\n   126\t\t\t\t\treturn\n   127\t\t\t\tcase <-heartbeat.C:\n   128\t\t\t\t\t// Select picks among ready cases at random: once stop has\n   129\t\t\t\t\t// asked, no further heartbeat starts, so it waits for at\n   130\t\t\t\t\t// most the one in flight (review round 1, HB01).\n   131\t\t\t\t\tselect {\n   132\t\t\t\t\tcase <-done:\n   133\t\t\t\t\t\treturn\n   134\t\t\t\t\tdefault:\n   135\t\t\t\t\t}\n   136\t\t\t\t\tbeatDelegationMarker(path, marker.RunToken)\n   137\t\t\t\tcase <-ticker.C:\n   138\t\t\t\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(request); err == nil {\n   139\t\t\t\t\t\tcancelled.Store(true)\n   140\t\t\t\t\t\tcancel()\n   141\t\t\t\t\t\treturn\n   142\t\t\t\t\t}\n   143\t\t\t\t}\n   144\t\t\t}\n   145\t\t}()\n   146\t\tvar once sync.Once\n   147\t\tstop = func(keepMarker ...bool) {\n   148\t\t\tonce.Do(func() {\n   149\t\t\t\tclose(done)\n   150\t\t\t\tdelegationStopRequested()\n   151\t\t\t\t// A heartbeat in flight finishes before the marker goes, so it\n   152\t\t\t\t// cannot rename a new one into place after the removal (spec\n   153\t\t\t\t// pose-delegation-marker-stop-joins-heartbeat R1).\n   154\t\t\t\t<-exited\n   155\t\t\t\t_ = os.Remove(request)\n   156\t\t\t\tif len(keepMarker) == 0 || !keepMarker[0] {\n   157\t\t\t\t\t_ = os.Remove(path)\n   158\t\t\t\t}\n   159\t\t\t})\n   160\t\t}\n   161\t\treturn stop, cancelled, nil\n   162\t}\n   163\t\n   164\t// delegationStopRequested is a test seam called once stop has asked the\n   165\t// watcher to end, before it waits for it.\n   166\tvar delegationStopRequested = func() {}\n   167\t\n   168\t// delegationHeartbeatEvery is the interval a running attempt declares.\n   169\tvar delegationHeartbeatEvery = 10 * time.Second\n   170\t\n   171\t// beatDelegationMarker rewrites the heartbeat of the marker run token names,\n   172\t// by compare-and-swap: a marker already recorded stalled, or replaced by\n   173\t// another run, is left as it is.\n   174\tfunc beatDelegationMarker(path, token string) {\n   175\t\traw, err := readLedgerRaw(path)\n   176\t\tif err != nil {\n   177\t\t\treturn\n   178\t\t}\n   179\t\tvar marker DelegationActiveRun\n   180\t\tif json.Unmarshal(raw, &marker) != nil || marker.RunToken != token {\n   181\t\t\treturn\n   182\t\t}\n   183\t\tmarker.HeartbeatAt = time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)\n   184\t\tnext, _ := json.MarshalIndent(marker, \"\", \"  \")\n   185\t\t_ = PublishFile(path, append(next, '\\n'), 0600, PublishAuthoritative, ExpectBytes(raw, true))\n   186\t}\n   187\t\n   188\t// delegationMarkerSilent reports a heartbeat older than three intervals.\n   189\tfunc delegationMarkerSilent(marker DelegationActiveRun, now time.Time) bool {\n   190\t\tevery := time.Duration(marker.HeartbeatEvery) * time.Second\n   191\t\tif every <= 0 {\n   192\t\t\tevery = delegationHeartbeatEvery\n   193\t\t}\n   194\t\tlast := marker.HeartbeatAt\n   195\t\tif last == \"\" {\n   196\t\t\tlast = marker.StartedAt\n   197\t\t}\n   198\t\tat, err := time.Parse(time.RFC3339Nano, last)\n   199\t\treturn err == nil && now.Sub(at) > 3*every\n   200\t}\n   201\t\n   202\t// stallClaimPath names the claim that records a run stalled.\n   203\tfunc stallClaimPath(dir string, marker DelegationActiveRun) string {\n   204\t\treturn filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\".\"+marker.RunToken+\".stalled\")\n   205\t}\n   206\t\n   207\tfunc readDelegationStall(dir string, marker DelegationActiveRun) string {\n   208\t\traw, err := readLedgerRaw(stallClaimPath(dir, marker))\n   209\t\tif err != nil {\n   210\t\t\treturn \"\"\n   211\t\t}\n   212\t\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(raw))\n   213\t}\n   214\t\n   215\t// recordDelegationStall publishes the stall claim exclusively: the first\n   216\t// reader's instant is the one every reader returns, so concurrent readers\n   217\t// agree (R4).\n   218\tfunc recordDelegationStall(dir string, marker DelegationActiveRun) string {\n   219\t\tat := time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)\n   220\t\t_ = PublishFile(stallClaimPath(dir, marker), []byte(at+\"\\n\"), 0600, PublishAuthoritative, PublishExpectation{Mode: ExpectAbsent})\n   221\t\treturn readDelegationStall(dir, marker)\n   222\t}\n   223\t\n   224\tfunc cancelRequestPath(dir string, marker DelegationActiveRun) string {\n   225\t\treturn filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\".\"+marker.RunToken+\".cancel\")\n   226\t}\n   227\t\n   228\tfunc validRunToken(token string) bool {\n   229\t\tif len(token) != 32 {\n   230\t\t\treturn false\n   231\t\t}\n   232\t\t_, err := hex.DecodeString(token)\n   233\t\treturn err == nil\n   234\t}\n   235\t\n   236\t// ListDelegationActive reads the markers with the liveness of their owners,\n   237\t// and returns as losses the markers it cannot read: a run it cannot observe is\n   238\t// reported, never listed as running nor left out in silence.\n   239\tfunc (s Store) ListDelegationActive() ([]DelegationActiveRun, []string, error) {\n   240\t\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(false)\n   241\t\tif errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n   242\t\t\treturn []DelegationActiveRun{}, nil, nil\n   243\t\t}\n   244\t\tif err != nil {\n   245\t\t\treturn nil, nil, err\n   246\t\t}\n   247\t\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(dir)\n   248\t\tif err != nil {\n   249\t\t\treturn nil, nil, err\n   250\t\t}\n   251\t\tout := []DelegationActiveRun{}\n   252\t\tvar lost []string\n   253\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n   254\t\t\tif !strings.HasSuffix(e.Name(), \".json\") {\n   255\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   256\t\t\t}\n   257\t\t\traw, err := readLedgerRaw(filepath.Join(dir, e.Name()))\n   258\t\t\tif errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n   259\t\t\t\tcontinue // the run ended between the listing and the read\n   260\t\t\t}\n   261\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   262\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the active run marker \"+e.Name()+\" could not be read: \"+err.Error())\n   263\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   264\t\t\t}\n   265\t\t\tvar marker DelegationActiveRun\n   266\t\t\tif json.Unmarshal(raw, &marker) != nil || marker.SchemaVersion != 1 || marker.AttemptID+\".json\" != e.Name() || !validRunToken(marker.RunToken) {\n   267\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the active run marker \"+e.Name()+\" is not one this engine reads\")\n   268\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   269\t\t\t}\n   270\t\t\tmarker.Liveness, marker.Detail = probeStartLockOwner(marker.Owner)\n   271\t\t\tmarker.StalledAt = readDelegationStall(dir, marker)\n   272\t\t\tif marker.StalledAt == \"\" && delegationMarkerSilent(marker, time.Now()) && marker.Liveness == LockOwnerDead {\n   273\t\t\t\tmarker.StalledAt = recordDelegationStall(dir, marker)\n   274\t\t\t}\n   275\t\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(cancelRequestPath(dir, marker)); err == nil {\n   276\t\t\t\tmarker.CancelRequested = true\n   277\t\t\t}\n   278\t\t\tout = append(out, marker)\n   279\t\t}\n   280\t\tsort.Slice(out, func(i, j int) bool {\n   281\t\t\treturn out[i].StartedAt < out[j].StartedAt || out[i].StartedAt == out[j].StartedAt && out[i].AttemptID < out[j].AttemptID\n   282\t\t})\n   283\t\treturn out, lost, nil\n   284\t}\n   285\t\n   286\t// ErrDelegationNotActive means no running run matches.\n   287\tvar ErrDelegationNotActive = errors.New(\"pose: the run is not running\")\n   288\t\n   289\t// RequestDelegationCancel asks the process running attempt to stop. A marker\n   290\t// whose owner is proven dead is not cancelled: nothing runs to stop.\n   291\tfunc (s Store) RequestDelegationCancel(attempt string) (DelegationActiveRun, error) {\n   292\t\tactive, _, err := s.ListDelegationActive()\n   293\t\tif err != nil {\n   294\t\t\treturn DelegationActiveRun{}, err\n   295\t\t}\n   296\t\tfor _, marker := range active {\n   297\t\t\tif marker.AttemptID != attempt {\n   298\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   299\t\t\t}\n   300\t\t\tif marker.Liveness == LockOwnerDead {\n   301\t\t\t\treturn marker, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: the process of attempt %s is gone (%s); nothing runs to cancel\", attempt, marker.Detail)\n   302\t\t\t}\n   303\t\t\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(false)\n   304\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   305\t\t\t\treturn marker, err\n   306\t\t\t}\n   307\t\t\trequest := cancelRequestPath(dir, marker)\n   308\t\t\tif err := PublishFile(request, []byte(time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339Nano)+\"\\n\"), 0600, PublishAuthoritative, PublishExpectation{Mode: ExpectAbsent}); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, os.ErrExist) {\n   309\t\t\t\tif _, statErr := os.Lstat(request); statErr != nil {\n   310\t\t\t\t\treturn marker, err\n   311\t\t\t\t}\n   312\t\t\t}\n   313\t\t\t// The run may have ended between the read and the request: a request\n   314\t\t\t// that names no running run is withdrawn, never left for a successor.\n   315\t\t\tif !s.DelegationRunActive(marker) {\n   316\t\t\t\t_ = os.Remove(request)\n   317\t\t\t\treturn marker, ErrDelegationNotActive\n   318\t\t\t}\n   319\t\t\tmarker.CancelRequested = true\n   320\t\t\treturn marker, nil\n   321\t\t}\n   322\t\treturn DelegationActiveRun{}, ErrDelegationNotActive\n   323\t}\n   324\t\n   325\t// DelegationRunActive reports whether the run marker names is still the\n   326\t// running run of its attempt.\n   327\tfunc (s Store) DelegationRunActive(marker DelegationActiveRun) bool {\n   328\t\tdir, err := s.delegationActiveDir(false)\n   329\t\tif err != nil {\n   330\t\t\treturn false\n   331\t\t}\n   332\t\traw, err := readLedgerRaw(filepath.Join(dir, marker.AttemptID+\".json\"))\n   333\t\tif err != nil {\n   334\t\t\treturn false\n   335\t\t}\n   336\t\tvar now DelegationActiveRun\n   337\t\treturn json.Unmarshal(raw, &now) == nil && now.RunToken == marker.RunToken\n   338\t}\n   339\t\n   340\t// DelegationPrivateDir is a directory of the engine's private delegation\n     1\t//go:build linux\n     2\t\n     3\tpackage pose\n     4\t\n     5\timport (\n     6\t\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n     7\t\t\"encoding/hex\"\n     8\t\t\"fmt\"\n     9\t\t\"os\"\n    10\t\t\"strings\"\n    11\t)\n    12\t\n    13\t// identifyStartLockProcess records the machine, boot, PID namespace and\n    14\t// process start time of the current process.\n    15\tfunc identifyStartLockProcess(owner *StartLockOwner) {\n    16\t\towner.MachineID = startLockMachineID()\n    17\t\towner.BootID = startLockBootID()\n    18\t\towner.PIDNamespace, _ = os.Readlink(\"/proc/self/ns/pid\")\n    19\t\towner.ProcessStart, _ = processStartTime(owner.PID)\n    20\t}\n    21\t\n    22\t// probeStartLockOwner says whether a lock owner is running. Only an owner\n    23\t// on this machine (same host and machine ID) can be judged: another boot of\n    24\t// it means the owner is gone; in the same boot and PID namespace, a PID that\n    25\t// is not running, or that runs a process started at another time, is not the\n    26\t// owner. Anything else — another host, no machine ID, another namespace, a\n    27\t// read that fails — is owner-unknown (R6).\n    28\tfunc probeStartLockOwner(owner StartLockOwner) (string, string) {\n    29\t\thost, _ := os.Hostname()\n    30\t\tmachine := startLockMachineID()\n    31\t\tif owner.Host != host || owner.MachineID == \"\" || owner.MachineID != machine {\n    32\t\t\treturn LockOwnerUnknown, fmt.Sprintf(\"held by pid %d on %s, another host or machine; its liveness cannot be checked here\", owner.PID, owner.Host)\n    33\t\t}\n    34\t\tboot := startLockBootID()\n    35\t\tif owner.BootID == \"\" || boot == \"\" {\n    36\t\t\treturn LockOwnerUnknown, \"the boot identity is unavailable\"\n    37\t\t}\n    38\t\tif owner.BootID != boot {\n    39\t\t\treturn LockOwnerDead, fmt.Sprintf(\"held by pid %d in an earlier boot of this machine\", owner.PID)\n    40\t\t}\n    41\t\tnamespace, _ := os.Readlink(\"/proc/self/ns/pid\")\n    42\t\tif owner.PIDNamespace == \"\" || owner.PIDNamespace != namespace {\n    43\t\t\treturn LockOwnerUnknown, fmt.Sprintf(\"held by pid %d in another PID namespace\", owner.PID)\n    44\t\t}\n    45\t\tif owner.PID <= 0 || owner.ProcessStart == \"\" {\n    46\t\t\treturn LockOwnerUnknown, \"the lock does not record the owner's process start\"\n    47\t\t}\n    48\t\tstarted, err := processStartTime(owner.PID)\n    49\t\tif processGone(err) {\n    50\t\t\treturn LockOwnerDead, fmt.Sprintf(\"pid %d is not running\", owner.PID)\n    51\t\t}\n    52\t\tif err != nil {\n    53\t\t\treturn LockOwnerUnknown, fmt.Sprintf(\"pid %d cannot be inspected: %v\", owner.PID, err)\n    54\t\t}\n    55\t\tif started != owner.ProcessStart {\n    56\t\t\treturn LockOwnerDead, fmt.Sprintf(\"pid %d now belongs to another process\", owner.PID)\n    57\t\t}\n    58\t\treturn LockOwnerActive, fmt.Sprintf(\"held by running pid %d (%s, since %s)\", owner.PID, owner.Operation, owner.AcquiredAt)\n    59\t}\n    60\t\n    61\t// processStartTime is field 22 of /proc/<pid>/stat: the start time of the\n    62\t// process in clock ticks since boot. It is read after the command name,\n    63\t// which may itself contain spaces and parentheses.\n    64\tfunc processStartTime(pid int) (string, error) {\n    65\t\traw, err := os.ReadFile(fmt.Sprintf(\"/proc/%d/stat\", pid))\n    66\t\tif err != nil {\n    67\t\t\treturn \"\", err\n    68\t\t}\n    69\t\ttext := string(raw)\n    70\t\tend := strings.LastIndexByte(text, ')')\n    71\t\tif end < 0 {\n    72\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"unreadable stat\")\n    73\t\t}\n    74\t\tfields := strings.Fields(text[end+1:])\n    75\t\t// fields[0] is field 3 (state); the start time is field 22.\n    76\t\tif len(fields) < 20 {\n    77\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"unreadable stat\")\n    78\t\t}\n    79\t\treturn fields[19], nil\n    80\t}\n    81\t\n    82\tfunc readTrimmed(path string) string {\n    83\t\traw, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n    84\t\tif err != nil {\n    85\t\t\treturn \"\"\n    86\t\t}\n    87\t\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(raw))\n    88\t}\n    89\t\n    90\t// startLockMachineID and startLockBootID are application-specific digests of\n    91\t// the machine and boot IDs: a lock file compares them without carrying the\n    92\t// machine ID, which systemd asks applications not to expose.\n    93\tfunc startLockMachineID() string { return appSpecificID(\"machine\", readTrimmed(\"/etc/machine-id\")) }\n    94\tfunc startLockBootID() string {\n    95\t\treturn appSpecificID(\"boot\", readTrimmed(\"/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id\"))\n    96\t}\n    97\t\n    98\tfunc appSpecificID(kind, raw string) string {\n    99\t\tif raw == \"\" {\n   100\t\t\treturn \"\"\n   101\t\t}\n   102\t\tsum := sha256.Sum256([]byte(\"pose-start-lock/\" + kind + \"/\" + raw))\n   103\t\treturn hex.EncodeToString(sum[:16])\n   104\t}\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:80:\t// (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R3).\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_brief.go:343:\t\tline(\"- Let every command you start finish before you end, and do not leave anything running in the background: POSE ends what is left and records it, and a long run left behind costs time you could have used.\")\nPOSE.md:357:  **Concurrency:** checks run up to `--jobs <n>` at a time (default: half the cores, bounded by available memory at about 1.5 GiB per check and by 8); results are reported and recorded in matrix order, whatever order they finish in, and the report records `jobs` and `wall_seconds`. `--jobs 1` and `--verbose` run one at a time. A check marked `exclusive: true` runs with nothing else running: earlier checks finish first, so its `when` can depend on them, which a concurrent check's cannot — a check's `when` is read as it starts. The run ends with its wall time beside the sum of its checks and the slowest checks. An interrupt or SIGTERM stops every running check's process group (on Windows, its process) and starts nothing more; those checks are recorded with `limit_state: cancelled`, the run is `fail` and exits 1, and a second signal acts at once. A default `--jobs` follows the cgroup's memory headroom and CPU quota where there is one.\nPOSE.md:401:- `close spec:<slug> --plan|--apply|--resume` (spec `pose-recoverable-closeout-plan`) — the closeout as an ordered, recoverable plan computed from the repository: context (an in-progress spec, no uncommitted change outside the paths the plan writes), evidence and index (validate into `results_path`, then `pose index`, as a pair), seal, mechanical attestation (`--reviewer` names the reviewing execution; never invented), judgment, verify and the guarded transition. `--plan` previews (`--json` too); `--apply [--digest <plan digest>]` refuses a plan that no longer matches the repository, runs the mechanical steps and stops with exit 3 at a pending judgment, listing it with the sealed evidence refs (`closeout_plan.evidence`) and an attest command filled with them, one `--tool` per required tool and one `--criterion` per pending judgment (`closeout_plan.attest`, spec `pose-quickstart-real-lifecycle`); a requirement trace that is incomplete — a requirement with no entry, an orphan or malformed entry, or requirements with no `### Requirement trace` — blocks the plan at its `trace` step, before anything is sealed, and `pose close` refuses the transition for the same reason, so a closed spec never fails the lint that follows; `--resume` re-plans after an interruption, so done steps are not repeated and no bundle or attestation is duplicated. A checkpoint under `.pose/closeout-plans/` records progress and is removed on completion; a removal that fails is reported. It is progress, never a receipt (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`): `--plan` shows it as `valid`, `corrupt`, `unsupported` or `unavailable`; an apply over one that does not read stops before any step, for a person to inspect and remove it; and one that records an evidence, index, seal, attestation or transition step the repository does not reflect is a conflict, until `--apply --digest <current plan>` starts a new run. The plan never answers a judgment, publishes or edits policy, and promises a recoverable operation, not a transaction over Git and external processes. Operation results (spec `pose-cli-mcp-operation-results`, ADR 2026-10-16): `--apply|--resume --json` writes exactly one JSON document — `operation`, `outcome` (`completed`, `already-satisfied`, `waiting`, `blocked`, `conflict`, `failed`), the `step` it stopped at, `steps_run`, `pending`, `next_action`, the `effects` the domain confirmed, the steps left `unconfirmed` after a failure, `resumable`, a typed `error.cause` and the plan in `data` — and the human output reads the same result. A call that runs no step reports `already-satisfied`, not `closed`: it is not a fresh verification, and `pose closeout-check` is. Two different modes, or `--digest`, `--reviewer` or `--skip-evidence` with `--plan`, exit 2 before anything runs; `pose start` refuses two of `--apply`, `--status` and `--cancel`, `--digest` outside `--apply` and `--cancel --json`; an apply that fails after recording its baseline reports the recorded phase as a confirmed effect and the same `--apply --digest` as its next action, which finishes from the start record a transition already published; a spec that has not moved is judged again by the current plan and its governed restrictions, and a baseline the repository has moved past is reported as a conflict to cancel and preview again, never as resumable; a failed apply lists every state it confirms as an effect (the baseline and, once published, the spec moved to `in-progress`) and in `steps_run` only the steps that call ran, and a start record left unreadable (a baseline write cut short) is reported with the `baseline` step unconfirmed and a next action to inspect and remove it, never repaired automatically. `pose close <scope>` without a plan mode refuses `--json`. An MCP gate (`pose_check`, `pose_lint_spec`, `pose_skills_check`) stopped by its deadline or a cancellation returns `isError` with `error_code: execution_inconclusive`, its cause and partial output, never `passed=false`. The usage journal records a stop on a reviewer as `waiting`, not `failed`, and never averages an unmeasured interval as 0 ms. The plan modes are a spec's: `pose close <scope> --plan|--apply|--resume` on a milestone or a roadmap exits 2 and says so. Any other scope closes with `pose close <scope>` and no plan flag (the guarded transition: it verifies the gate, which on a large repository takes minutes, and names the next closeout, `pose close roadmap:<slug>` once every milestone of a roadmap is closed); `pose closeout-check <scope>` reads whether it is terminal and is what `terminal=true` means for a milestone or a roadmap.\nPOSE.md:423:- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle. `discover`, `integrate` and `tech-debt` take `--no-persist` to print their result without writing `.pose/assessments/` or `.pose/state/`; in the sealed checkout of a delegated review (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) they never persist, `init`, `snapshot` and `request` refuse, and the review plan runs them with `--no-persist`, so a reviewer running a planned assessment does not fail its own run.\nPOSE.md:427:- `start` — atomic start of a draft spec, journaled in a per-spec start record. Without flags it is a read-only, digest-bound preview: readiness and dependencies, the declared review obligations (no future structural delta needed) and the R/A/D baseline. `--apply --digest <sha256>` records that baseline and moves the spec from `draft` to `in-progress` under a per-spec lock with compare-and-swap; it needs `atomic_start_version: 1` in the review policy, repeats idempotently, resumes an interrupted run and refuses another plan for a started spec. `--cancel` undoes a start that never moved the spec. The per-spec lock records its owner (host, digests of the machine and boot IDs, PID namespace, PID and process start); a lock another call holds is reported, never broken by age, as `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` or `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Dead is proven only on the same Linux machine — another boot, or a PID that is not running or now belongs to another process — and `--recover-lock <owner digest>`, with the digest `--status` shows, removes that lock only if its bytes and its dead owner are unchanged, leaving the start record to resume; an unknown owner (another host, an older engine's empty lock, another platform) is checked and removed by a person. The same lock is the spec's lifecycle lock: close and transfer take it too, so no two of them interleave on one spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifies each node as `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` or `legacy-unbaselined`, recomputes obligations and, under the capability, asks for reconciliation when status or the recorded baseline was edited by hand. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limits the capability to specs created on or after that date: an older spec in progress without a start is reported as `legacy-unbaselined` with a note and blocks nothing; without the date every such spec needs reconciliation. A start records observable precedence, not when anything was decided; MCP exposes the read-only view as `pose_start_status`.\nPOSE.md:907:The sandboxed Claude review profile that pose-dist qualified for its own reviews uses safe/restricted mode, Read/Grep/Glob/Bash only, mandatory shell sandboxing (`failIfUnavailable:true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands:false`), and a read-only sealed source tree. Both fresh and resumed wrappers require bubblewrap, socat and jq on Linux (jq remains required on macOS), with installation advice on refusal. Go cache/temp writes go to private operation-owned scratch outside the checkout; the existing module cache is read, and unavailable downloads/tests that write into source can fail. The profile instructs the reviewer to run `go clean -cache` as its final Bash command inside the sandbox; the engine independently retains incomplete cleanup as a failed run under its unchanged limits. Never clean an adapter-controlled cache outside the sandbox: a test can replace its path with a symlink. This qualifies Claude's shell subprocess boundary, not isolation of the trusted Claude executable or arbitrary credential reads. Checkout integrity checks still apply. Installing engine code does not adopt the profile in another instance; copy and qualify its explicit policy block before use. The wrapper also denies the resolved physical Git common directory, because Claude auto-allows it for worktrees. For this opt-in profile, POSE prepares only empty `.claude/.cc-writes` directories at the disposable checkout root and declared components before taking its integrity snapshot; added files and all later mutations still fail. Final sandboxed cleanup includes leftover `go-build*` directories directly under GOTMPDIR after all jobs finish.\nPOSE.md:909:`pose delegate ls` lists attempts by their latest run with state, purpose, agent, label, turns, age and last step — for an interrupted run also its cause, whether a partial draft exists, the reset instant and the time left — and the runs in progress with the liveness of their process. `pose delegate cancel <run>` stops a run in progress; it is recorded `failed` with the cause `cancelled`, and its attempt stays consumed. `pose delegate agents` lists each agent with vendor, model, whether its CLI is on the path, the reset instant of its last recorded usage limit and the state routing reads: `available`, `limited until <instant>` (its last run hit a usage limit whose reset is ahead), `missing` (its CLI is not on the path), `unknown` (the run history could not be read whole) or `probe-failed` (its optional `probe` failed within its bound). Availability is read from the path and the recorded runs; only a configured probe runs anything. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` starts the run as a process of its own, in a session of its own that survives the caller's terminal, and returns at once with the attempt (a run reference) and the path of its log; a run whose route must first wait for a usage-limit reset is not detached. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` is a plain foreground process that ends when the run does and prints one line — run, state, decision, resumable flag, reset instant — with the exit code `0` approved, `10` changes requested or rejected, `20` failed (a completed run with no decision too), `30` interrupted, `40` stalled, `50` the wait timed out, and `2` for a usage error; every waiter is released, a run already ended returns at once, and killing a wait leaves the run running. The process running an attempt rewrites a heartbeat in its marker every 10 seconds; a run whose heartbeat is older than three intervals and whose process is provably gone is recorded `stalled` by the first reader, through an exclusive claim every reader then agrees on, and `ls` shows each running run's heartbeat age. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` shows an attempt as one task: the runs it recorded are replayed with a marker where a run resumed another and the interrupted run naming the run that continued it, then a running attempt is followed through the normalized, redacted events its process writes as they come, until the record is published; `--no-follow` prints what exists and exits, `-v` adds the tail of each tool's output (`--lines <n>`, default 12) and `--json` prints NDJSON. With no reference it follows the only running run; at a terminal it otherwise opens fzf with a preview of the run under the cursor refreshed every `--refresh <d>` (default 2s, 0 turns it off), or a numbered menu without fzf; outside a terminal an absent reference exits 2 and lists the candidates. Colours appear only on a terminal and follow `NO_COLOR`. A live event is redacted with the credentials and the session references the stream has declared so far; the events bound to the record are derived again from the whole transcript. `on_terminal` on an agent (or else on a purpose) is a command run once after a run ends, with only `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` and `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` in its environment, the run directory as working directory, its own process group and a bound (`timeout`, default 30s, at most 5m); its failure is recorded beside the run, shown by `ls`, and never changes the run's state. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto>` continues a run that stopped interrupted or failed, short of a conclusion, with another agent under the same attempt and its continuation budget, which is the `resume.max_resumes` and `max_wall_time` of the agent that opened the attempt (3 and 60 minutes by default) whichever agent continues it, so changing agents never widens it; a completed run, the same agent and a run already continued are refused, and a run handed off is no longer resumed. Its brief is the predecessor's generated brief followed by a labelled section: the predecessor's partial final message and the commands it ran with their exit status, paired by the call identifier the agent's stream records and otherwise left unknown rather than guessed, each with its digest, quoted so no line of it reads as a decision, bounded at 16 KiB with the cut disclosed, and an instruction to re-verify. Only the handoff run concludes. The record binds the base brief digest and the section digest in `handoff_digest`, names its predecessor in `hands_off_from`, and a signed proof verifies the section against the predecessor's record. `--to auto` routes as the purpose does, the stopped agent excluded and unavailable ones skipped. A foreground run or handoff that stops interrupted in a terminal offers waiting for its reset and resuming, handing off, or cancelling, which is offered even alone, and does nothing until answered; the stopping command's result, `ls --json` and `wait --json` carry the same ways forward as `next_actions`, each with its command, whether it is available now — judged by the planning its command runs, budget and wait bound included — and why not, and a `cancel` that leaves the run stopped. `watch` marks a handoff with `⇄` beside the `↻` of a resume. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` hands any task to another agent: the prompt goes on stdin in `--cd` (default: the current directory) with the agent's command for `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; the default is `purposes.task.default_access` (read when unset), `full` is never a default and prints a warning naming what it disables. A task's template places `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` and `{outside_git}`, filled from the agent's `add_dir_args` (with `{dir}`), `no_network_args` and `outside_git_args`; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` and `--effort` that the agent's template cannot carry are refused with exit 2, and `--no-network` with `write` or `full` too. The final message goes to stdout and the agent's exit code is returned; `--json` replaces both with one document. A task runs in the real directory: containment bounds its time and output, not its filesystem. Its record (prompt, stream, error stream, normalized events, argv, removed variable names, exit code, label) is owner-only under the user's state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, where `XDG_STATE_HOME` counts only as an absolute path and `~/.local/state` is the default), or, with `--record`, in the `delegate-runs` directory of `.pose`. The state directory may be reached through a link of the user's own; resolved, it and every directory above it must belong to the user or to root and be writable by no group or other user unless sticky, and `pose`, `pose/delegate` and the project's directory below it must be real directories of the user's that no group or other user can write; a task refuses to record or read anywhere else and names the directory (on Windows only links are refused). `--detach` starts a task as the review purposes start a detached run and returns its attempt, its log and the place of its record; a prompt piped to `task -` or `resume --prompt -` is read before detaching and written to the detached run through a pipe, never a file, and a refusal before the attempt is reserved returns the foreground's document and exit code. A detached call keeps its log and hand-back in `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, which, with every directory up to the project root, must be the user's and writable by no group or other user, and whose root's ancestors must belong to the user or root and be writable by no one else unless sticky; the root is resolved once and only that resolved root is used, a caller whose current directory is gone is refused, and a project's private task history is keyed by its resolved root (records an earlier engine kept under a linked spelling are still read). A detached call believes a hand-back only once a piped prompt is through and a run reserved the attempt it names. A checkout that fails this can still run in the foreground; on Windows only links and non-directories are refused, and ACLs are not checked. A task is never evidence: no review reader reads it, `pose review attest --run` refuses it by name, and `wait` returns 0 when the agent succeeded, with no decision. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` and `handoff` work on tasks; `resume` without `--prompt` sends a default continuation message, with the agent's `resume.commands` for the task's access, and `--agent auto` routes against the launching session. A task can also continue a session POSE never recorded, such as one a personal launcher opened, with `--session <id> --agent <name>`: the agent's resume command for the access runs with that session, the prompt optional, and the record says it continued a session without keeping it. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` and `status`, the aliases this surface deprecated, were removed: each exits 2 and names its `pose delegate` replacement.\nPOSE.md:954:**Optional worktree runtime.** `pose runtime start` starts, detached, a runtime for this worktree; `attach` and `status` handshake with it, `stop` drains it (`--drain <d>`, default 5s) and says how many requests in flight finished and how many were abandoned, `restart` is stop then start, and `serve` runs it in the foreground (`--idle <d>`, default 30m, stops an idle runtime; 0 never does). It is opt-in: no other command starts it, and every command runs standalone whether it runs or not. The runtime is identified by the user, the canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) and the Git common directory, so two worktrees of one repository, or two users, never share one; the handshake compares the engine, build, protocol and schema as well and an attach from another engine is refused naming the field. Its endpoint is a Unix domain socket (Linux, macOS) of mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, else `pose-runtime-<uid>` under the temporary directory, or `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), whose peer the operating system identifies before a byte is read; a link, a loose mode, another owner or a file that is not a socket is refused, and a socket nobody answers on is replaced. There is no TCP and no token. Slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers handshake, status and stop, confers no authority, signature or presence, and every restart has a new epoch, inheriting nothing. On Windows, which has no named-pipe implementation here, `status` reports `unsupported` and every command runs standalone. `status` exits 0 for running, standalone and unsupported and 1 for an incompatible runtime; `attach` also exits 1 when there is nothing to attach to. `--json` prints the document `pose.runtime.status/1`.\nPOSE.md:1001:Declare an optional `containment` object on each agent in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`. Use containment schema 1 and `trusted-process`. This backend runs a trusted local executable; a disposable worktree shares Git metadata and does not isolate filesystem, original repository, network, credential files, CPU or memory. Required guarantees that the host cannot enforce are refused before reserving an attempt or spawning a process. Linux process-group termination requires an observable process inventory and is checked after kill; descendants that escape the group remain outside its coverage. Windows/other ports do not qualify group containment from a cross-build.\nPOSE.md:1078:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter's descendants are not ended at all, which the run record states. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:142:  `[wont-do: <motivo>]`. Isso responde, por follow-up, se ele foi reaproveitado\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:182:   (`[spawned]`/`[covered]`/`[duplicate]`) — reaproveitar follow-up é decisão,\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:359:  **Checks carregados:** `--carry --json-out <results_path>` renova o resultado canônico em vez de rodar a matriz inteira de novo. Ele usa o resultado canônico atual como base quando a base é comparável: mesmo schema, modo, digest da matriz e `engine_version` (a versão do motor mais a revisão VCS da qual o binário foi compilado; um build de árvore modificada nunca carrega), sem filtro, não cancelada (um sinal cancela o run inteiro, mesmo um só com checks carregados), com `material_clean` registrado (validou uma cópia de trabalho inalterada do próprio head: nenhum material rastreado ou não rastreado e não ignorado mudou, e o HEAD não se moveu durante o run, segundo o reflog dele e de todo submódulo; mudanças de modo contam qualquer que seja `core.fileMode`, e todo submódulo precisa estar no commit registrado, sem submódulo aninhado, verificado antes da leitura do plano e no fim do run; uma spec cujo material o motor não consegue calcular continua indisponível em qualquer run limpo e não recusa a base), com `git_head` ancestral de `HEAD`, cópia de trabalho limpa agora nos paths materiais sistema de arquivos que diferencia maiúsculas, sem diretório com casefold no caminho até um módulo ou uma dependência, nenhum módulo alcançado por symlink ou compartilhando diretório com outro módulo, nenhum symlink rastreado cujo path ou alvo esteja num módulo que não seja a raiz ou num `dependsOn` declarado (ou cujo alvo saia do repositório), inclusive na árvore de todo submódulo (um submódulo sem checkout ou com submódulos aninhados recusa), nenhuma dependência declarada fora do repositório ou escrita como path não limpo ou que resolve por symlink (rastreado ou ignorado), apenas paths ASCII de módulo e dependência, nenhuma entrada de índice marcada assume-unchanged ou skip-worktree, e nenhum id de check definido duas vezes e metadados de módulo da cópia de trabalho iguais aos do HEAD. Os nomes alterados são lidos com todo gitlink visível, qualquer que seja a configuração do Git. O head é fixado antes do planejamento, e um run cujo HEAD se moveu registra seus bindings de material como indisponíveis. Roda os módulos que as regras de escopo por mudança selecionam entre o head da base e `HEAD`; o arquivo do resultado canônico e os registros administrativos derivados que o binding de material ignora (arquivos de dados ainda regulares nos diretórios de registros derivados sob `.pose/`: índices, resultados, estado, assessments e registros de revisão; a matriz de validação continua material) não contam como mudança. Um módulo cujos checks não estão todos na base também roda. Todo outro check mantém o resultado da base, inclusive o desfecho, e registra `carried_from` (o head em que rodou, mantido em carregamentos encadeados) e `carried_generated_at`; o índice de entrega lê esse head no resultado, então o frescor da evidência julga o commit que um check carregado realmente observou. Uma falha carregada continua reprovando o run. Qualquer condição não atendida roda tudo, e `carry.reason` diz por quê. `carry` registra a base, o número de arquivos alterados e o motivo de cada módulo selecionado, e `counts.carried` os checks reaproveitados. `--carry` não combina com `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--root-only`, `--changed-from` ou `--changed-to`.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:361:  **Concorrência:** os checks rodam até `--jobs <n>` ao mesmo tempo (padrão: metade dos núcleos, limitada pela memória disponível, cerca de 1,5 GiB por check, e por 8); os resultados são mostrados e registrados na ordem da matriz, seja qual for a ordem em que terminem, e o relatório registra `jobs` e `wall_seconds`. `--jobs 1` e `--verbose` rodam um de cada vez. Um check marcado `exclusive: true` roda sem nenhum outro em execução: os checks anteriores terminam antes, então seu `when` pode depender deles, o que o de um check concorrente não pode — o `when` de um check é lido quando ele vai começar. A execução termina com seu tempo de parede ao lado da soma dos checks e os checks mais lentos. Uma interrupção ou SIGTERM encerra o grupo de processos de cada check em execução (no Windows, seu processo) e não inicia mais nada; esses checks são registrados com `limit_state: cancelled`, a execução fica `fail` e sai com 1, e um segundo sinal age na hora. O `--jobs` padrão segue a folga de memória e a cota de CPU do cgroup, quando há um.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:374:- `followups` — agrega os follow-ups de `Final Report > Follow-ups` de todas as specs, deriva o backlog vivo (`--open`, default) ou completo (`--all`), projeta titularidade (`--owner <alias>`) e reviews vencidas (`--overdue`), e propõe **candidatos a near-duplicate** por similaridade léxica determinística (Jaccard de tokens + `SequenceMatcher`, stdlib; limiar via `--similarity 0..100`, default 60). Exit 0 por padrão, sem rede; `--fail-overdue` transforma reviews vencidas em gate de política bloqueante baseado em risco. Os candidatos são pistas mecânicas — o **julgamento semântico** e a **confirmação de reaproveitamento** vivem na camada de agente (skill `pose-spec-closeout`), nunca neste script. Cobertura das fontes (spec `pose-source-coverage-integrity`): a leitura informa o que não pôde ver. `--json` traz `coverage_complete` e `coverage` (cada perda nomeia o artefato, o tipo e a spec a que pertence quando o nome do arquivo ou um cabeçalho legível mostra), a saída humana imprime `coverage=INCOMPLETE` e cada arquivo não lido, e `--fail-overdue` falha em vez de certificar \"nenhum vencido\" sobre uma leitura incompleta. O backlog, `--candidates`, o estado do projeto e `pose state --attention` contam um único conjunto não resolvido: um `[open]` explícito e um bullet sem disposição (o Attention projeta o segundo como `followup-undisposed`). O mesmo vale para `pose assess tech-debt`, cujo relatório declara os arquivos varridos, os que não pôde ler (incompleto) e os que deixa de fora de propósito (acima do limite de tamanho, binários, gerados), e acha um marcador depois de uma linha muito longa; e para o histórico de reviews: `pose review-check` e `pose review verify` não tomam a última tentativa ou atestação legível como a corrente enquanto existir um registro ilegível que possa ser posterior, e `pose review record` recusa escolher a tentativa que substitui a partir de um histórico com buraco. O relatório de obrigações marca um produtor como `incomplete` quando ele perdeu um elemento (uma obrigação recusada pela validação, uma spec ou um registro de review que não pôde ler).\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:406:- Reuso por equivalência material (spec `pose-material-equivalence-reuse`) — a resposta de um critério só é reaproveitada quando todo insumo do qual ela depende é igual no novo bundle: a definição, a independência, as seções de escopo, as entradas do assunto (todas, para um critério sensível ao assunto), a evidência das classes que ele aceita, os insumos de governança e suas ferramentas; reuso de julgamento também exige `allow_criterion_reuse`. Quando um bundle é substituído, `review verify` (`delta.criterion_reuse` no JSON) mostra cada critério como `equivalent`, `changed` com os insumos que mudaram, ou `new`, e cada resposta reaproveitada nomeia a attestation de origem. Editar registro operacional como o log de Validation não muda nenhum critério; uma mudança de código nomeia o assunto alterado.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:407:- Frescor de assessment (spec `pose-adaptive-assessment-freshness`) — um assessment de componente registra seu vínculo: a árvore commitada do componente, a versão do motor e o digest da matriz de validação. `pose assess discover --if-stale [--component <dir>]` reaproveita um assessment cujo vínculo não mudou e o refaz caso contrário, mostrando `assess.freshness.<componente>` com o motivo (conteúdo mudou, motor mudou, matriz mudou, mudanças não commitadas, ou registro antigo sem vínculo). Idade sozinha nunca torna um assessment stale. Um assessment ausente ou stale de um componente declarado por uma spec em andamento aparece como obrigação consultiva de `evidence` em `state --attention`; não bloqueia nada.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:427:- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — motores nativos de assessment para LOC, marcadores de débito técnico, estruturas de componentes, checagens de integridade de contrato entre módulos e ciclo de vida de reavaliação de capabilities. `discover`, `integrate` e `tech-debt` aceitam `--no-persist` para imprimir o resultado sem gravar `.pose/assessments/` nem `.pose/state/`; no checkout selado de uma review delegada (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) eles nunca gravam, `init`, `snapshot` e `request` recusam, e o plano de review os roda com `--no-persist`, para que um revisor que roda um assessment planejado não derrube o próprio run.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:431:- `start` — início atômico de uma spec draft, registrado num start record por spec. Sem flags é um preview somente leitura vinculado a digest: readiness e dependências, as obrigações de review declaradas (sem exigir structural delta futuro) e a baseline R/A/D. `--apply --digest <sha256>` grava essa baseline e move a spec de `draft` para `in-progress` com lock por spec e compare-and-swap; exige `atomic_start_version: 1` na policy de review, repete de forma idempotente, retoma uma execução interrompida e recusa outro plano para spec já iniciada. `--cancel` desfaz um start que não moveu a spec. A trava por spec registra seu dono (host, digests dos IDs de máquina e de boot, namespace de PID, PID e início do processo); uma trava de outra chamada é informada, nunca quebrada pela idade, como `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` ou `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Morte só é provada na mesma máquina Linux — outro boot, ou um PID que não roda ou agora é de outro processo — e `--recover-lock <digest do dono>`, com o digest que `--status` mostra, remove essa trava só se os bytes e o dono morto não mudaram, deixando o start record para retomar; um dono desconhecido (outro host, a trava vazia de um motor anterior, outra plataforma) é verificado e removido por uma pessoa. A mesma trava é a trava de ciclo de vida da spec: close e transfer também a tomam, então dois deles nunca se intercalam numa spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifica cada nó como `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` ou `legacy-unbaselined`, recalcula as obrigações e, com a capability, pede reconciliação quando status ou baseline registrada foram editados à mão. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limita a capability às specs criadas a partir dessa data: uma spec mais antiga em andamento sem start aparece como `legacy-unbaselined` com uma nota e não bloqueia nada; sem a data, toda spec assim exige reconciliação. Um start registra precedência observável, não quando algo foi decidido; o MCP expõe a visão somente leitura como `pose_start_status`.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:902:O perfil de revisão Claude sandboxed que o pose-dist qualificou para as próprias revisões usa safe/restricted, somente Read/Grep/Glob/Bash, sandbox obrigatório dos comandos (`failIfUnavailable:true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands:false`) e source selado sem escrita. Os wrappers fresh e resume exigem bubblewrap, socat e jq no Linux (jq também no macOS), com dica de instalação na recusa. Cache/temp do Go ficam em scratch privado da operação fora do checkout; o cache de módulos existente é lido, e downloads indisponíveis ou testes que escrevem no source podem falhar. O perfil instrui o revisor a rodar `go clean -cache` como último comando Bash dentro do sandbox; o motor confere a limpeza independentemente, com os limites inalterados, e mantém limpeza incompleta como falha. Nunca limpar o cache controlado pelo adaptador fora do sandbox: um teste pode substituir seu caminho por um symlink. A qualificação cobre a fronteira dos subprocessos shell do Claude, não isolamento do executável confiável nem sigilo de credenciais arbitrárias. Checks de integridade do checkout continuam obrigatórios. Instalar o motor não adota o perfil em outra instância; copie e qualifique o bloco explícito da policy antes de usá-lo. O wrapper também nega o Git comum físico resolvido, pois o Claude o permite automaticamente em worktrees. Para esse perfil opt-in, o POSE prepara somente diretórios vazios `.claude/.cc-writes` na raiz descartável e nos componentes declarados antes do snapshot; arquivos novos e alterações posteriores continuam recusados. A limpeza final dentro do sandbox também remove somente diretórios `go-build*` diretamente em GOTMPDIR, depois de todos os jobs terminarem.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:904:`pose delegate ls` lista as tentativas pelo run mais recente com estado, propósito, agente, label, turnos, idade e último passo — para um run interrompido também a causa, se há draft parcial, o instante de reset e o tempo restante — e os runs em andamento com a vivacidade do processo. `pose delegate cancel <run>` para um run em andamento; ele é registrado `failed` com a causa `cancelled`, e a tentativa continua consumida. `pose delegate agents` lista cada agente com vendor, modelo, se a CLI está no path, o instante de reset do último limite de uso registrado e o estado que o roteamento lê: `available`, `limited until <instante>` (o último run atingiu um limite de uso cujo reset ainda não chegou), `missing` (a CLI não está no path), `unknown` (o histórico de runs não pôde ser lido inteiro) ou `probe-failed` (o `probe` opcional falhou dentro do seu limite). A disponibilidade é lida do path e dos runs registrados; só um probe configurado executa algo. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` inicia o run como um processo próprio, numa sessão própria que sobrevive ao terminal de quem chamou, e retorna na hora com a tentativa (uma referência de run) e o caminho do seu log; um run cuja rota precisa antes esperar o reset de um limite de uso não é destacado. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` é um processo comum em primeiro plano que termina quando o run termina e imprime uma linha — run, estado, decisão, se é retomável, instante de reset — com o código de saída `0` aprovado, `10` mudanças pedidas ou rejeitado, `20` falhou (também um run concluído sem decisão), `30` interrompido, `40` travado (`stalled`), `50` a espera esgotou o tempo, e `2` para erro de uso; todos os que esperam são liberados, um run já encerrado retorna na hora, e matar um wait deixa o run rodando. O processo que roda uma tentativa reescreve um heartbeat no seu marcador a cada 10 segundos; um run cujo heartbeat tem mais de três intervalos e cujo processo comprovadamente não existe mais é registrado `stalled` pelo primeiro leitor, por uma reivindicação exclusiva com que todos os leitores concordam, e `ls` mostra a idade do heartbeat de cada run em andamento. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` mostra uma tentativa como uma tarefa só: os runs registrados são reproduzidos com um marcador onde um run retomou outro e o run interrompido nomeando o run que o continuou, e uma tentativa em andamento é então acompanhada pelos eventos normalizados e redigidos que o seu processo grava à medida que chegam, até o registro ser publicado; `--no-follow` imprime o que existe e sai, `-v` acrescenta o fim da saída de cada ferramenta (`--lines <n>`, padrão 12) e `--json` imprime NDJSON. Sem referência ele acompanha o único run em andamento; num terminal, caso contrário, abre o fzf com um preview do run sob o cursor atualizado a cada `--refresh <d>` (padrão 2s, 0 desliga), ou um menu numerado sem fzf; fora de um terminal uma referência ausente sai com 2 e lista os candidatos. Cores só aparecem num terminal e seguem `NO_COLOR`. Um evento ao vivo é redigido com as credenciais e as referências de sessão que o stream declarou até ali; os eventos vinculados ao registro são derivados de novo do transcript inteiro. `on_terminal` num agente (ou, senão, num propósito) é um comando executado uma vez depois que um run termina, só com `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` e `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` no ambiente, o diretório de runs como diretório de trabalho, um grupo de processos próprio e um limite (`timeout`, padrão 30s, no máximo 5m); a falha dele é registrada ao lado do run, mostrada pelo `ls`, e nunca muda o estado do run. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agente|auto>` continua um run que parou interrompido ou falho, sem conclusão, com outro agente na mesma tentativa e no seu orçamento de continuação, que é o `resume.max_resumes` e o `max_wall_time` do agente que abriu a tentativa (3 e 60 minutos por padrão), qualquer que seja o agente que a continua, de modo que trocar de agente nunca o amplia; um run concluído, o mesmo agente e um run já continuado são recusados, e um run repassado não é mais retomado. O brief é o brief gerado do predecessor seguido de uma seção rotulada: a mensagem final parcial do predecessor e os comandos que ele rodou com o status de saída, pareados pelo identificador de chamada que o stream do agente registra e, sem ele, deixados como desconhecidos em vez de adivinhados, cada um com seu digest, citados para que nenhuma linha vire uma decisão, limitada a 16 KiB com o corte declarado, e uma instrução de reverificar. Só o run de handoff conclui. O registro vincula o digest do brief base e o da seção em `handoff_digest`, nomeia o predecessor em `hands_off_from`, e uma prova assinada verifica a seção contra o registro do predecessor. `--to auto` roteia como o propósito, excluindo o agente parado e pulando os indisponíveis. Um run ou handoff em primeiro plano que para interrompido num terminal oferece esperar o reset e retomar, fazer o handoff ou cancelar, oferecido mesmo sozinho, e não faz nada até a resposta; o resultado do comando que parou, `ls --json` e `wait --json` trazem os mesmos caminhos em `next_actions`, cada um com o comando, se está disponível agora — avaliado pelo planejamento que o comando roda, orçamento e limite de espera incluídos — e por que não, e um `cancel` que deixa o run parado. `watch` marca um handoff com `⇄`, ao lado do `↻` de uma retomada. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` entrega qualquer tarefa a outro agente: o prompt vai pelo stdin em `--cd` (padrão: o diretório atual) com o comando do agente para `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; o padrão é `purposes.task.default_access` (read quando ausente), `full` nunca é padrão e imprime um aviso dizendo o que ele desliga. O template de uma task posiciona `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` e `{outside_git}`, preenchidos a partir de `add_dir_args` (com `{dir}`), `no_network_args` e `outside_git_args` do agente; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` e `--effort` que o template do agente não comporta são recusados com saída 2, assim como `--no-network` com `write` ou `full`. A mensagem final vai para o stdout e o código de saída do agente é devolvido; `--json` troca os dois por um documento. Uma task roda no diretório real: a contenção limita seu tempo e sua saída, não seu sistema de arquivos. O registro (prompt, stream, stream de erro, eventos normalizados, argv, nomes das variáveis removidas, código de saída, label) fica só para o dono no diretório de estado do usuário (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, em que `XDG_STATE_HOME` só vale como caminho absoluto e o padrão é `~/.local/state`), ou, com `--record`, no diretório `delegate-runs` de `.pose`. O diretório de estado pode ser alcançado por um link do próprio usuário; resolvido, ele e todo diretório acima dele devem pertencer ao usuário ou ao root e não ser graváveis por grupo nem por outros usuários, salvo com sticky, e `pose`, `pose/delegate` e o diretório do projeto abaixo dele devem ser diretórios reais do usuário que nenhum grupo ou outro usuário possa gravar; uma task se recusa a registrar ou ler em qualquer outro lugar e nomeia o diretório (no Windows só links são recusados). `--detach` inicia uma task como os propósitos de review iniciam um run destacado e devolve a tentativa, o log e o lugar do registro; um prompt enviado por pipe a `task -` ou `resume --prompt -` é lido antes de destacar e escrito no run destacado por um pipe, nunca por um arquivo, e uma recusa antes de a tentativa ser reservada devolve o documento e o código de saída do primeiro plano. Uma chamada destacada guarda o log e a devolução em `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, que, com todo diretório até a raiz do projeto, deve ser do usuário e não gravável por grupo nem por outros usuários, e cujos ancestrais da raiz devem pertencer ao usuário ou ao root e não ser graváveis por mais ninguém, salvo com sticky; a raiz é resolvida uma vez e só essa raiz resolvida é usada, quem chama a partir de um diretório que não existe mais é recusado, e o histórico privado de tasks de um projeto é indexado pela raiz resolvida (registros que um motor anterior guardou sob uma grafia com link continuam sendo lidos). Uma chamada destacada só acredita numa devolução depois que o prompt enviado por pipe passou e um run reservou a tentativa que ela nomeia. Um checkout que não atende a isso ainda roda em primeiro plano; no Windows só links e não diretórios são recusados, e ACLs não são verificadas. Uma task nunca é evidência: nenhum leitor de review a lê, `pose review attest --run` a recusa pelo nome, e `wait` devolve 0 quando o agente teve sucesso, sem decisão. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` e `handoff` funcionam com tasks; `resume` sem `--prompt` envia uma mensagem padrão de continuação, com os `resume.commands` do agente para o acesso da task, e `--agent auto` roteia contra a sessão que a lançou. Uma task também pode continuar uma sessão que o POSE nunca registrou, como uma que um launcher pessoal abriu, com `--session <id> --agent <nome>`: o comando de resume do agente para o acesso roda com essa sessão, o prompt é opcional, e o registro diz que continuou uma sessão sem guardá-la. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` e `status`, os aliases que esta superfície depreciou, foram removidos: cada um sai com 2 e nomeia o substituto em `pose delegate`.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:933:2. Substitua cada uso: uma review por `pose delegate review`, uma adjudicação por `pose delegate adjudicate`, qualquer outra tarefa por `pose delegate task`, uma continuação de um run que o POSE registrou por `pose delegate resume` ou `pose delegate handoff` (eles reaproveitam o agente, o acesso e os settings dele), uma sessão que o launcher deixou interrompida por `pose delegate task --session <id> --agent <nome>` — passando os settings que o launcher usou (`--access`, `--cd`, `--add-dir`, `--effort`, `--no-network`), que o POSE não consegue recuperar de uma sessão que nunca registrou; o run que ele registra então é retomado por `pose delegate resume`, reaproveitando-os — um run em segundo plano por `--detach` e `pose delegate wait`, o acompanhamento por `pose delegate watch` e a listagem por `pose delegate ls`.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:949:**Runtime opcional por worktree.** `pose runtime start` inicia, destacado, um runtime para este worktree; `attach` e `status` fazem o handshake com ele, `stop` o drena (`--drain <d>`, padrão 5s) e diz quantas requisições em voo terminaram e quantas foram abandonadas, `restart` é stop e depois start, e `serve` o roda em primeiro plano (`--idle <d>`, padrão 30m, para um runtime ocioso; 0 nunca para). É opt-in: nenhum outro comando o inicia, e todo comando roda standalone com ou sem ele. O runtime é identificado pelo usuário, pelo worktree canônico (links resolvidos) e pelo diretório comum do Git, de modo que dois worktrees de um repositório, ou dois usuários, nunca compartilham um; o handshake compara também engine, build, protocolo e schema, e um attach de outro engine é recusado nomeando o campo. O endpoint é um socket de domínio Unix (Linux, macOS) com modo 0600 num diretório 0700 do usuário (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, senão `pose-runtime-<uid>` sob o diretório temporário, ou `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), cujo par o sistema operacional identifica antes de se ler um byte; um link, um modo frouxo, outro dono ou um arquivo que não é socket são recusados, e um socket em que ninguém responde é substituído. Não há TCP nem token. A fatia 1 não hospeda nenhuma operação do domínio: responde handshake, status e stop, não confere autoridade, assinatura nem presença, e cada restart tem uma época nova, sem herdar nada. No Windows, que não tem implementação de named pipe aqui, `status` informa `unsupported` e todo comando roda standalone. `status` sai com 0 para running, standalone e unsupported e com 1 para um runtime incompatível; `attach` também sai com 1 quando não há o que anexar. `--json` imprime o documento `pose.runtime.status/1`.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:998:Declare `containment` opcional em cada agente de `.pose/policy/delegation.json`, contenção schema 1 e `trusted-process`. O backend exige executável local confiável. Worktree compartilha Git; não isola filesystem, raiz original, rede, credenciais em disco, CPU ou memória. Exigência indisponível é recusada antes da reserva/spawn. Linux qualifica término do grupo somente com inventário observável e verificação após kill. Filho que escapa do grupo fica fora dessa cobertura; cross-build não qualifica runtime Windows/outras plataformas.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux o envio roda o adaptador sob um shim de run de review, o mesmo executável reentrado, que é o subreaper da subárvore do run: depois que o adaptador termina, num timeout e quando o envio termina, ele encerra e recolhe todo processo que restou nessa subárvore, mesmo um que abriu sessão própria e limpou o ambiente, e relata os pids encerrados por um pipe que o revisor não herda; o envio os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza. Quem embute o motor sem ativar o shim recorre a encerrar os processos cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); nas outras plataformas Unix só o grupo de processos é encerrado, e no Windows os descendentes do adaptador não são encerrados, o que o registro do run declara. O brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, sob um lock que o sistema libera junto com quem o detém, e uma liberação conclui com qualquer run que a tentativa já registrou em vez de gravar outro. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:357:  **Concurrency:** checks run up to `--jobs <n>` at a time (default: half the cores, bounded by available memory at about 1.5 GiB per check and by 8); results are reported and recorded in matrix order, whatever order they finish in, and the report records `jobs` and `wall_seconds`. `--jobs 1` and `--verbose` run one at a time. A check marked `exclusive: true` runs with nothing else running: earlier checks finish first, so its `when` can depend on them, which a concurrent check's cannot — a check's `when` is read as it starts. The run ends with its wall time beside the sum of its checks and the slowest checks. An interrupt or SIGTERM stops every running check's process group (on Windows, its process) and starts nothing more; those checks are recorded with `limit_state: cancelled`, the run is `fail` and exits 1, and a second signal acts at once. A default `--jobs` follows the cgroup's memory headroom and CPU quota where there is one.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:401:- `close spec:<slug> --plan|--apply|--resume` (spec `pose-recoverable-closeout-plan`) — the closeout as an ordered, recoverable plan computed from the repository: context (an in-progress spec, no uncommitted change outside the paths the plan writes), evidence and index (validate into `results_path`, then `pose index`, as a pair), seal, mechanical attestation (`--reviewer` names the reviewing execution; never invented), judgment, verify and the guarded transition. `--plan` previews (`--json` too); `--apply [--digest <plan digest>]` refuses a plan that no longer matches the repository, runs the mechanical steps and stops with exit 3 at a pending judgment, listing it with the sealed evidence refs (`closeout_plan.evidence`) and an attest command filled with them, one `--tool` per required tool and one `--criterion` per pending judgment (`closeout_plan.attest`, spec `pose-quickstart-real-lifecycle`); a requirement trace that is incomplete — a requirement with no entry, an orphan or malformed entry, or requirements with no `### Requirement trace` — blocks the plan at its `trace` step, before anything is sealed, and `pose close` refuses the transition for the same reason, so a closed spec never fails the lint that follows; `--resume` re-plans after an interruption, so done steps are not repeated and no bundle or attestation is duplicated. A checkpoint under `.pose/closeout-plans/` records progress and is removed on completion; a removal that fails is reported. It is progress, never a receipt (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`): `--plan` shows it as `valid`, `corrupt`, `unsupported` or `unavailable`; an apply over one that does not read stops before any step, for a person to inspect and remove it; and one that records an evidence, index, seal, attestation or transition step the repository does not reflect is a conflict, until `--apply --digest <current plan>` starts a new run. The plan never answers a judgment, publishes or edits policy, and promises a recoverable operation, not a transaction over Git and external processes. Operation results (spec `pose-cli-mcp-operation-results`, ADR 2026-10-16): `--apply|--resume --json` writes exactly one JSON document — `operation`, `outcome` (`completed`, `already-satisfied`, `waiting`, `blocked`, `conflict`, `failed`), the `step` it stopped at, `steps_run`, `pending`, `next_action`, the `effects` the domain confirmed, the steps left `unconfirmed` after a failure, `resumable`, a typed `error.cause` and the plan in `data` — and the human output reads the same result. A call that runs no step reports `already-satisfied`, not `closed`: it is not a fresh verification, and `pose closeout-check` is. Two different modes, or `--digest`, `--reviewer` or `--skip-evidence` with `--plan`, exit 2 before anything runs; `pose start` refuses two of `--apply`, `--status` and `--cancel`, `--digest` outside `--apply` and `--cancel --json`; an apply that fails after recording its baseline reports the recorded phase as a confirmed effect and the same `--apply --digest` as its next action, which finishes from the start record a transition already published; a spec that has not moved is judged again by the current plan and its governed restrictions, and a baseline the repository has moved past is reported as a conflict to cancel and preview again, never as resumable; a failed apply lists every state it confirms as an effect (the baseline and, once published, the spec moved to `in-progress`) and in `steps_run` only the steps that call ran, and a start record left unreadable (a baseline write cut short) is reported with the `baseline` step unconfirmed and a next action to inspect and remove it, never repaired automatically. `pose close <scope>` without a plan mode refuses `--json`. An MCP gate (`pose_check`, `pose_lint_spec`, `pose_skills_check`) stopped by its deadline or a cancellation returns `isError` with `error_code: execution_inconclusive`, its cause and partial output, never `passed=false`. The usage journal records a stop on a reviewer as `waiting`, not `failed`, and never averages an unmeasured interval as 0 ms. The plan modes are a spec's: `pose close <scope> --plan|--apply|--resume` on a milestone or a roadmap exits 2 and says so. Any other scope closes with `pose close <scope>` and no plan flag (the guarded transition: it verifies the gate, which on a large repository takes minutes, and names the next closeout, `pose close roadmap:<slug>` once every milestone of a roadmap is closed); `pose closeout-check <scope>` reads whether it is terminal and is what `terminal=true` means for a milestone or a roadmap.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:423:- `assess discover|integrate|tech-debt|stale|request|snapshot` — native assessment engines for LOC, technical debt markers, component structures, inter-module contract integrity checks, and capability mechanism reassessment lifecycle. `discover`, `integrate` and `tech-debt` take `--no-persist` to print their result without writing `.pose/assessments/` or `.pose/state/`; in the sealed checkout of a delegated review (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`) they never persist, `init`, `snapshot` and `request` refuse, and the review plan runs them with `--no-persist`, so a reviewer running a planned assessment does not fail its own run.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:427:- `start` — atomic start of a draft spec, journaled in a per-spec start record. Without flags it is a read-only, digest-bound preview: readiness and dependencies, the declared review obligations (no future structural delta needed) and the R/A/D baseline. `--apply --digest <sha256>` records that baseline and moves the spec from `draft` to `in-progress` under a per-spec lock with compare-and-swap; it needs `atomic_start_version: 1` in the review policy, repeats idempotently, resumes an interrupted run and refuses another plan for a started spec. `--cancel` undoes a start that never moved the spec. The per-spec lock records its owner (host, digests of the machine and boot IDs, PID namespace, PID and process start); a lock another call holds is reported, never broken by age, as `owner-active`, `owner-unknown` or `owner-dead` (spec `pose-journal-checkpoint-and-lock-recovery`). Dead is proven only on the same Linux machine — another boot, or a PID that is not running or now belongs to another process — and `--recover-lock <owner digest>`, with the digest `--status` shows, removes that lock only if its bytes and its dead owner are unchanged, leaving the start record to resume; an unknown owner (another host, an older engine's empty lock, another platform) is checked and removed by a person. The same lock is the spec's lifecycle lock: close and transfer take it too, so no two of them interleave on one spec (spec `pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication`). `--status` classifies each node as `recorded-before-managed-execution`, `introduced-during-execution` or `legacy-unbaselined`, recomputes obligations and, under the capability, asks for reconciliation when status or the recorded baseline was edited by hand. `atomic_start_adopted_at: YYYY-MM-DD` (spec `pose-atomic-start-adoption-cutoff`) limits the capability to specs created on or after that date: an older spec in progress without a start is reported as `legacy-unbaselined` with a note and blocks nothing; without the date every such spec needs reconciliation. A start records observable precedence, not when anything was decided; MCP exposes the read-only view as `pose_start_status`.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:894:The sandboxed Claude review profile that pose-dist qualified for its own reviews uses safe/restricted mode, Read/Grep/Glob/Bash only, mandatory shell sandboxing (`failIfUnavailable:true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands:false`), and a read-only sealed source tree. Both fresh and resumed wrappers require bubblewrap, socat and jq on Linux (jq remains required on macOS), with installation advice on refusal. Go cache/temp writes go to private operation-owned scratch outside the checkout; the existing module cache is read, and unavailable downloads/tests that write into source can fail. The profile instructs the reviewer to run `go clean -cache` as its final Bash command inside the sandbox; the engine independently retains incomplete cleanup as a failed run under its unchanged limits. Never clean an adapter-controlled cache outside the sandbox: a test can replace its path with a symlink. This qualifies Claude's shell subprocess boundary, not isolation of the trusted Claude executable or arbitrary credential reads. Checkout integrity checks still apply. Installing engine code does not adopt the profile in another instance; copy and qualify its explicit policy block before use. The wrapper also denies the resolved physical Git common directory, because Claude auto-allows it for worktrees. For this opt-in profile, POSE prepares only empty `.claude/.cc-writes` directories at the disposable checkout root and declared components before taking its integrity snapshot; added files and all later mutations still fail. Final sandboxed cleanup includes leftover `go-build*` directories directly under GOTMPDIR after all jobs finish.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:896:`pose delegate ls` lists attempts by their latest run with state, purpose, agent, label, turns, age and last step — for an interrupted run also its cause, whether a partial draft exists, the reset instant and the time left — and the runs in progress with the liveness of their process. `pose delegate cancel <run>` stops a run in progress; it is recorded `failed` with the cause `cancelled`, and its attempt stays consumed. `pose delegate agents` lists each agent with vendor, model, whether its CLI is on the path, the reset instant of its last recorded usage limit and the state routing reads: `available`, `limited until <instant>` (its last run hit a usage limit whose reset is ahead), `missing` (its CLI is not on the path), `unknown` (the run history could not be read whole) or `probe-failed` (its optional `probe` failed within its bound). Availability is read from the path and the recorded runs; only a configured probe runs anything. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` starts the run as a process of its own, in a session of its own that survives the caller's terminal, and returns at once with the attempt (a run reference) and the path of its log; a run whose route must first wait for a usage-limit reset is not detached. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` is a plain foreground process that ends when the run does and prints one line — run, state, decision, resumable flag, reset instant — with the exit code `0` approved, `10` changes requested or rejected, `20` failed (a completed run with no decision too), `30` interrupted, `40` stalled, `50` the wait timed out, and `2` for a usage error; every waiter is released, a run already ended returns at once, and killing a wait leaves the run running. The process running an attempt rewrites a heartbeat in its marker every 10 seconds; a run whose heartbeat is older than three intervals and whose process is provably gone is recorded `stalled` by the first reader, through an exclusive claim every reader then agrees on, and `ls` shows each running run's heartbeat age. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` shows an attempt as one task: the runs it recorded are replayed with a marker where a run resumed another and the interrupted run naming the run that continued it, then a running attempt is followed through the normalized, redacted events its process writes as they come, until the record is published; `--no-follow` prints what exists and exits, `-v` adds the tail of each tool's output (`--lines <n>`, default 12) and `--json` prints NDJSON. With no reference it follows the only running run; at a terminal it otherwise opens fzf with a preview of the run under the cursor refreshed every `--refresh <d>` (default 2s, 0 turns it off), or a numbered menu without fzf; outside a terminal an absent reference exits 2 and lists the candidates. Colours appear only on a terminal and follow `NO_COLOR`. A live event is redacted with the credentials and the session references the stream has declared so far; the events bound to the record are derived again from the whole transcript. `on_terminal` on an agent (or else on a purpose) is a command run once after a run ends, with only `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` and `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` in its environment, the run directory as working directory, its own process group and a bound (`timeout`, default 30s, at most 5m); its failure is recorded beside the run, shown by `ls`, and never changes the run's state. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto>` continues a run that stopped interrupted or failed, short of a conclusion, with another agent under the same attempt and its continuation budget, which is the `resume.max_resumes` and `max_wall_time` of the agent that opened the attempt (3 and 60 minutes by default) whichever agent continues it, so changing agents never widens it; a completed run, the same agent and a run already continued are refused, and a run handed off is no longer resumed. Its brief is the predecessor's generated brief followed by a labelled section: the predecessor's partial final message and the commands it ran with their exit status, paired by the call identifier the agent's stream records and otherwise left unknown rather than guessed, each with its digest, quoted so no line of it reads as a decision, bounded at 16 KiB with the cut disclosed, and an instruction to re-verify. Only the handoff run concludes. The record binds the base brief digest and the section digest in `handoff_digest`, names its predecessor in `hands_off_from`, and a signed proof verifies the section against the predecessor's record. `--to auto` routes as the purpose does, the stopped agent excluded and unavailable ones skipped. A foreground run or handoff that stops interrupted in a terminal offers waiting for its reset and resuming, handing off, or cancelling, which is offered even alone, and does nothing until answered; the stopping command's result, `ls --json` and `wait --json` carry the same ways forward as `next_actions`, each with its command, whether it is available now — judged by the planning its command runs, budget and wait bound included — and why not, and a `cancel` that leaves the run stopped. `watch` marks a handoff with `⇄` beside the `↻` of a resume. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` hands any task to another agent: the prompt goes on stdin in `--cd` (default: the current directory) with the agent's command for `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; the default is `purposes.task.default_access` (read when unset), `full` is never a default and prints a warning naming what it disables. A task's template places `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` and `{outside_git}`, filled from the agent's `add_dir_args` (with `{dir}`), `no_network_args` and `outside_git_args`; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` and `--effort` that the agent's template cannot carry are refused with exit 2, and `--no-network` with `write` or `full` too. The final message goes to stdout and the agent's exit code is returned; `--json` replaces both with one document. A task runs in the real directory: containment bounds its time and output, not its filesystem. Its record (prompt, stream, error stream, normalized events, argv, removed variable names, exit code, label) is owner-only under the user's state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, where `XDG_STATE_HOME` counts only as an absolute path and `~/.local/state` is the default), or, with `--record`, in the `delegate-runs` directory of `.pose`. The state directory may be reached through a link of the user's own; resolved, it and every directory above it must belong to the user or to root and be writable by no group or other user unless sticky, and `pose`, `pose/delegate` and the project's directory below it must be real directories of the user's that no group or other user can write; a task refuses to record or read anywhere else and names the directory (on Windows only links are refused). `--detach` starts a task as the review purposes start a detached run and returns its attempt, its log and the place of its record; a prompt piped to `task -` or `resume --prompt -` is read before detaching and written to the detached run through a pipe, never a file, and a refusal before the attempt is reserved returns the foreground's document and exit code. A detached call keeps its log and hand-back in `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, which, with every directory up to the project root, must be the user's and writable by no group or other user, and whose root's ancestors must belong to the user or root and be writable by no one else unless sticky; the root is resolved once and only that resolved root is used, a caller whose current directory is gone is refused, and a project's private task history is keyed by its resolved root (records an earlier engine kept under a linked spelling are still read). A detached call believes a hand-back only once a piped prompt is through and a run reserved the attempt it names. A checkout that fails this can still run in the foreground; on Windows only links and non-directories are refused, and ACLs are not checked. A task is never evidence: no review reader reads it, `pose review attest --run` refuses it by name, and `wait` returns 0 when the agent succeeded, with no decision. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` and `handoff` work on tasks; `resume` without `--prompt` sends a default continuation message, with the agent's `resume.commands` for the task's access, and `--agent auto` routes against the launching session. A task can also continue a session POSE never recorded, such as one a personal launcher opened, with `--session <id> --agent <name>`: the agent's resume command for the access runs with that session, the prompt optional, and the record says it continued a session without keeping it. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` and `status`, the aliases this surface deprecated, were removed: each exits 2 and names its `pose delegate` replacement.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:941:**Optional worktree runtime.** `pose runtime start` starts, detached, a runtime for this worktree; `attach` and `status` handshake with it, `stop` drains it (`--drain <d>`, default 5s) and says how many requests in flight finished and how many were abandoned, `restart` is stop then start, and `serve` runs it in the foreground (`--idle <d>`, default 30m, stops an idle runtime; 0 never does). It is opt-in: no other command starts it, and every command runs standalone whether it runs or not. The runtime is identified by the user, the canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) and the Git common directory, so two worktrees of one repository, or two users, never share one; the handshake compares the engine, build, protocol and schema as well and an attach from another engine is refused naming the field. Its endpoint is a Unix domain socket (Linux, macOS) of mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, else `pose-runtime-<uid>` under the temporary directory, or `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), whose peer the operating system identifies before a byte is read; a link, a loose mode, another owner or a file that is not a socket is refused, and a socket nobody answers on is replaced. There is no TCP and no token. Slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers handshake, status and stop, confers no authority, signature or presence, and every restart has a new epoch, inheriting nothing. On Windows, which has no named-pipe implementation here, `status` reports `unsupported` and every command runs standalone. `status` exits 0 for running, standalone and unsupported and 1 for an incompatible runtime; `attach` also exits 1 when there is nothing to attach to. `--json` prints the document `pose.runtime.status/1`.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:988:Declare an optional `containment` object on each agent in `.pose/policy/delegation.json`. Use containment schema 1 and `trusted-process`. This backend runs a trusted local executable; a disposable worktree shares Git metadata and does not isolate filesystem, original repository, network, credential files, CPU or memory. Required guarantees that the host cannot enforce are refused before reserving an attempt or spawning a process. Linux process-group termination requires an observable process inventory and is checked after kill; descendants that escape the group remain outside its coverage. Windows/other ports do not qualify group containment from a cross-build.\npose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:1065:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter's descendants are not ended at all, which the run record states. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
